QSORT(3)                       P32W Unix                        QSORT(3)

NAME
       qsort, bsearch - sort and search an array

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdlib.h>

       void qsort(void *base, size_t n, size_t size,
                  int (*cmp)(const void *, const void *));
       void *bsearch(const void *key, const void *base, size_t n,
                  size_t size, int (*cmp)(const void *, const void *));

DESCRIPTION
       qsort sorts the array of n elements of size bytes each at
       base into ascending order. cmp is called with pointers to two
       elements and must return a negative number, 0 or a positive
       number as the first is less than, equal to or greater than
       the second.

       bsearch looks for an element equal to *key in the array at
       base, which must already be sorted by cmp, by binary search.
       cmp is called with key as its first argument and a pointer to
       an element as its second.

       qsort is Quicksort. The pivot is the median of the first,
       middle and last elements; the partition stops at elements
       equal to the pivot from both sides, so many equal keys split
       evenly; and the smaller part is sorted first, by recursion,
       and the larger by a loop, so the stack is at most log2 n
       calls deep. Sorted, reversed and all-equal input take time
       proportional to n log n.

RETURN VALUE
       bsearch returns a pointer to a matching element (any one, if
       several match), or NULL.

EXAMPLES
       int bynum(const void *a, const void *b)
       {
           return *(const int *)a - *(const int *)b;
       }
       ...
       qsort(v, n, sizeof v[0], bynum);

       static int bystr(const void *a, const void *b)
       {   /* for an array of char *: */
           return strcmp(*(char **)a, *(char **)b);
       }

SEE ALSO
       sort(1), strcmp(3), stdlib(3)

BUGS
       The sort is not stable: equal elements end up in no
       particular order. A specially built input can still take
       time proportional to n squared (as for any Quicksort with
       this pivot rule). The subtraction in bynum above can
       overflow for values more than 2^31 apart.
